--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 1 - SUMMARY OF THE PAPER 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Large language model (LLM) agents have moved from research demos toward
genuine tools used inside companies, but the path from "an LLM that can call
a function" to "an agent that pays for itself inside a real business
workflow" is still unevenly mapped. Tang and co-authors try to draw that
map. They argue that industry agents - autonomous or semi-autonomous systems
deployed in concrete business contexts - face problems that general agents
do not: real time constraints in finance, real authority constraints in
healthcare, and real physical constraints in manufacturing. Existing
surveys, they note, mostly look at each piece (memory, planning, tools) in
isolation. This one tries to put them back together with industrial
deployment as the organising frame.

The survey's central contribution is a five-level capability maturity
model that lines up agent sophistication with the technologies that make
each level possible:

  L1 - Process Execution System
       Translate natural language to a structured action; extract
       information from documents. Deterministic, scripted, RPA-like.

  L2 - Interactive Problem-Solving System
       Augment a human in the loop, support decisions, single tool calls.
       Most "copilot" products live here.

  L3 - End-to-End Autonomous System
       Multi-step planning, persistent memory, recovery from errors.
       The current frontier in coding agents, AI-scientist systems, and
       embodied robotics.

  L4 - Collaborative Intelligent System
       Multi-agent teams, shared state, simulation of whole systems
       (markets, traffic, supply chains).

  L5 - Adaptive Social System
       Self-improving population of agents that generate their own goals
       and evolve across "generations." Today, this is a thin frontier
       with only a handful of prototypes.

The authors then track three technology pillars - Memory, Planning, and
Tool Use - through the same five rungs. The argument is that each rung of
the maturity model is gated by a matching upgrade in each pillar:

  Memory
    L1 Contextual          - just the LLM's context window
    L2 Passive Retrieval   - retrieval-augmented generation over an
                             external knowledge base
    L3 Active Learning     - reflection, experience internalisation
    L4 Distributed Shared  - shared memory across multiple agents
    L5 Evolutionary        - memory carried across generations of agents

  Planning
    L1 Linear              - chain-of-thought
    L2 Reactive            - ReAct-style think/act/observe loops
    L3 Global              - tree-of-thought, look-ahead, world models
    L4 Collaborative       - multi-agent role assignment, task allocation
    L5 Autonomous Goal     - self-directed, open-ended goal generation

  Tool Use
    L1 Instruction-Driven  - one-shot, program-aided language models
    L2 Goal-Driven         - learned function calling (Toolformer, ReAct)
    L3 Dynamic Orchestration - tool selection and routing at runtime
    L4 Distributed Management - tool marketplaces, agent-as-tool patterns
    L5 Tool Creation       - agents that synthesise their own tools

After grounding the framework, the survey walks through where each
maturity level actually shows up in practice. L1 covers NL-to-SQL and
document extraction. L2 covers copilots and GUI agents. L3 is where most
current industrial work sits - code agents, AI scientists, embodied
robotics. L4 contains agentic trading, customer service, and city-scale
simulators. L5 is a thin frontier - the paper itself lists only two
examples in Fig. 2 (EvoAgent and AdaSociety).

The evaluation chapter is the most practically useful part of the paper.
The authors split benchmarks into two layers: fundamental benchmarks (one
per pillar - Memory, Planning, Tool Use) and industry-specific benchmarks
(general agent suites such as AgentBench versus domain-specific suites
such as WebArena or VisualWebArena). They are explicit that current
benchmarks under-test the things industry actually cares about:
long-horizon reliability, safety under adversarial users, regulatory
compliance, and the authenticity of the evaluation environment.

The paper closes on bottlenecks. They do not split evenly across the
three pillars:
  - In Memory, the hard part is long-context forgetting and filtering
    noisy environmental information into structured experience.
  - In Planning, the hard part is brittleness under real-world dynamism
    - real environments break static-world assumptions.
  - In Tool Use, the hard part is selecting, combining, and recovering
    from tools as toolboxes grow into hundreds of APIs.

Above the technology layer, the survey points to governance, capability
boundaries, and the cost of injecting deep domain expertise as the
defining questions for the next generation of industry deployments.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 2 - MY MEDIUM ARTICLE (in my own words)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Title:    From Lab Demos to Production Lines: A Field Guide to LLM-Driven
          Industry Agents
Subtitle: A walk-through of the October 2025 survey by Tang et al. - and
          what it tells us about why most agent demos never become
          products.
Length:   ~3,500 words / ~12 min read.


1. TL;DR
--------

LLM agents - systems that plan, call tools, and act over long horizons -
are the most-hyped corner of AI in 2025, but most of them never leave the
demo reel. Tang et al. try to explain why, and propose a clean framework
for thinking about *industrial* agents specifically: not the ones that win
benchmarks, but the ones that quietly do work inside companies. The
mental model the paper asks you to adopt is this:

   An industry agent is not a model. It is a workflow - built on the
   three pillars of Memory, Planning, and Tool Use - measured by
   maturity, not accuracy.


2. Why I picked this paper
--------------------------

There is no shortage of agent surveys in 2025. What makes this one
different is its audience. Most surveys are written by researchers, for
researchers. They taxonomise prompting tricks and benchmark scores and
stop at the point where a CEO actually has to make a decision. Tang et
al. begin where those surveys stop. They reframe the question from "how
smart can it be?" into "how dependable can it be?" - and dependability
is what separates a viral Twitter video from a line item on a P&L.


3. The central question
-----------------------

The whole survey, compressed into one line:

   "LLM agents can reason, plan and execute tasks autonomously - but how
   do we translate that capability into industrial productivity that
   actually transforms how work gets done?"

The rest of the 60+ pages answer it by doing three things: defining a
maturity ladder, decomposing the architecture into three reusable
pillars, and mapping the five industry domains where agents are actually
deployed today.


4. The gap nobody talks about
-----------------------------

Researchers and practitioners optimise for different objectives, and
this survey is unusually honest about it.

What papers tend to optimise for:
   - Accuracy on a curated benchmark.
   - Short-horizon, single-turn reasoning.
   - Closed, simulated tool sets.
   - Aggregate scores rather than workflow value.
   - Few constraints on cost, latency, or governance.

What real deployments actually need:
   - End-to-end completion of multi-step workflows.
   - Long-horizon memory that survives across sessions and quarters.
   - Open tool ecosystems - CRMs, data warehouses, legacy ERPs.
   - SLA-grade reliability, observability, and audit trails.
   - Compliance, safety boundaries, and human-in-the-loop overrides.

The contribution of this survey is to refuse to pick a side. It treats
both columns as inputs to a single design problem.


5. The capability maturity framework
------------------------------------

The headline contribution is the L1->L5 ladder (Process Execution ->
Reactive Assistant -> Autonomous Worker -> Collaborative Agent ->
Adaptive Social System).

The framework is useful in two ways. First, it gives a vocabulary for
honest conversations - when a vendor says "our agent automates your
sales pipeline," you can ask: at what level? Second, it implies that L5
is not always the goal. A compliance team might never want anything
above L3, because predictability is itself a feature.

My take: this is the most underrated idea in the paper. The field
collectively rewards going "higher" - bigger models, longer horizons,
more autonomy - but a lot of real value lives at L2 and L3.
Acknowledging that openly is, weirdly, one of the more grown-up moves
I have seen in an agent paper this year.


6. The three pillars
--------------------

   Pillar 1 - Memory
   Working memory, episodic memory, semantic memory, procedural
   memory. The single most important shift: retrieval is now a
   first-class citizen. Production memory is a vector store plus a
   symbolic index plus a ranker - not a longer context window.
   Reflection (agents summarising their own traces) is what keeps
   memory from growing faster than time. And governance lives here:
   PII, retention windows, access control must be enforced at the
   layer where data persists, not at the application layer above it.

   Pillar 2 - Planning
   Four broad strategies that production systems blend rather than
   choose between:
      - Chain-of-Thought for narrow, well-scoped subtasks.
      - ReAct loops for any agent with a tool surface.
      - Tree or graph search for risky or irreversible actions.
      - Reflection and replanning for recovery from failure.

   Pillar 3 - Tool Use
   The canonical closed loop is:
      LLM Core -> Tool Router -> Tool Ecosystem -> Observation ->
      Memory Update -> back to the LLM.
   Three practical lessons:
      - Tool surface equals attack surface. Sandboxing and scoped
        credentials are the work, not the optional polish.
      - Latency is a product feature. Tool batching, caching, and async
        I/O separate "magical" from "abandoned after a week."
      - Schemas beat free text. Strict typed function signatures cut
        hallucinated tool calls dramatically.


7. Where industry agents are actually landing
---------------------------------------------

Five domains where deployment is real, not aspirational:
   1. Digital engineering - coding copilots, autonomous SWE agents,
      on-call copilots. The most mature domain by a wide margin.
   2. Scientific discovery - literature review, hypothesis generation,
      lab control, paper drafting. High promise, very long horizons.
   3. Embodied intelligence - robotics, navigation, manipulation.
   4. Collaborative business execution - customer experience, sales,
      operations. Agents alongside human teams, not replacing them.
   5. Complex system simulation - markets, traffic, supply chains,
      social dynamics.

Spotlight 1 - Digital engineering. Where industry agents are most
credible today. Software has rich digital traces, clear success
signals (the tests pass or they don't), API-first tooling, and safe
sandboxes. If you want to see an L4 agent in the wild, watch a modern
autonomous coding agent handle a multi-file refactor.

Spotlight 2 - Scientific discovery. The opposite - promising but hard.
Long horizons, expensive experiments, novelty poorly captured by
accuracy. The interesting work today is workflow assistance, not full
autonomy.


8. Evaluation: beyond accuracy
------------------------------

Industry agents need three layers of evaluation working together, not
one leaderboard:

   1. Fundamental capabilities - reasoning, tool use, planning, memory.
   2. Specialised benchmarks - per-domain suites for software, science,
      web, embodied tasks.
   3. Real-world impact - workflow completion rate, cost per task,
      end-to-end latency, user trust, and regret.

A model that scores 90% on a benchmark but takes 90 seconds and $40 to
answer a single customer question is a worse product than a model that
scores 70% in one second for one cent. The current eval suites
over-index on reasoning and tool use, and under-measure memory, safety,
and cost.

My take: the agent space is heading for a metric crisis similar to the
one that hit recommender systems a decade ago - benchmark wins climb
while user-facing quality plateaus. The way out is multi-layer
evaluation, exactly as the authors propose.


9. Open challenges
------------------

Six bottlenecks blocking the move from L3 toward L4 and L5:
   - Authenticity: benchmarks miss the noise of real workflows.
   - Safety and governance: still bolted on, not designed in.
   - Long-horizon memory: what to remember, forget, surface.
   - Cost and latency: agent loops multiply tokens.
   - Multi-agent coordination: role assignment and shared state at
     scale.
   - Domain adaptation: re-tuning per vertical without redoing
     everything.

None are surprises individually; bundling them as one roadmap is the
useful part.


10. My critical take on the survey
----------------------------------

Strengths:
   - First survey I have seen that centres maturity rather than model
     size.
   - Bridges agent research with concrete industrial pillars in a way
     engineering teams actually think.
   - Treats evaluation as multi-layer.
   - Explicit that safety and governance are core, not optional.

Where it could go deeper:
   - Quantitative cross-domain comparisons are sparse.
   - Cost and latency engineering - arguably the biggest demo-vs-
     product gap - gets less ink than it deserves.
   - Multi-agent governance is described as a challenge but not
     seriously benchmarked.
   - The open-source vs. proprietary agent gap is under-discussed; in
     2025-26 this will dominate enterprise decisions.

None are fatal. They are the natural limits of a synthesis paper
written in a moving field.


11. Five things to remember
---------------------------

   1. Industry agents are a category, not a model. The unit of analysis
      is the workflow, not the LLM call.
   2. Memory + Planning + Tools is the canonical stack. Every
      production design re-derives these three pillars whether they
      acknowledge them or not.
   3. Maturity, not accuracy, is the right yardstick. L1 -> L5
      captures organisational fit far better than benchmark numbers.
   4. Evaluation must be multi-layered. Capability scores, domain
      benchmarks, and real-world impact metrics belong together.
   5. Governance is the new performance. Auditability, safety, and
      cost are what decide what actually ships.


